A||||12||Shawn Alexander||lowering
Z||000000||Shawn Alexander||03-01-99||03:06 PM||||Cliff,<br>   How did you go about lowering your<br>car and how is the ride? Is it a stock<br>body?
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||03-01-99||04:09 PM||||Yes, it is a stock body. I didn't do the lowering myself (don't have a garage to do it in) but had it done by the Fiero Factory here in Holland (not affiliated with Fiero Factory in the U.S.). I only had the springs replaced because my Fiero already had pretty good Koni shocks.<p>The disadvantage of lowering it so much is that you now feel every small bump in the road and even the smallest hole in the road sounds like you've just been hit by a truck...<p>The really big advantage is that it did wonders for high-speed stability. Before I lowered it, I never took my Fiero over 90mph because it would go all over the place and it was very sensitive to sidewinds. Now, I've took it to about 115mph and it still is a very smooth ride.<p>And it looks sooooo sweet! It's probably one of the lowest Fiero's here in Holland. I've seen hundreds of them (on shows and meetings), but none of them were as low as mine. And it wasn't really expensive. It costed me around $400.- (including labor) but those were very well spent! If you want, I can send you a picture by email.
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||03-02-99||03:40 PM||||Hi Cliff,<p>I wouldn't mind seeing a photo of your Fiero as well.<p>thanks!<p>(email is steve@primeline.net)
Z||000003||Shawn Alexander||03-02-99||04:04 PM||||Cliff,<br>    I would like to see a picture<br>of your car.<br>Shawn
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||03-03-99||03:34 AM||||Ok, I'll get out my digital cam and snap a few shots! (as soon as it stops raining, that is!)
Z||000005||John Johnston||03-04-99||12:14 AM||||Cliff, when you lowered your car, did you have to make some adjustments to the camber/caster? I would imagine that operation of the suspension would be thrown off by the lower springs.
Z||000006||John Johnston||03-04-99||12:17 AM||||Cliff, I wonder if your high-speed stability could have been affected by the fact that you are running 205s all around. Mine came from the factory with 215s in the back. What do you think?
Z||000007||Shawn Alexander||03-05-99||09:37 AM||||I'm not 100% sure on this but i think you can<br>purchase an upper control arm that makes<br>up the difference in caster/camber change<br>when lowering the car. I also think you <br>can buy them thruogh local pontiac dealer.<br>Shawn
Z||000008||Shawn Alexander||03-06-99||09:02 AM||||I did find some info on the A-arms<br>in a may of '95 issue of kit car<br>mag and they highly recomend installing<br>these A-arms. They claim they have 1.6<br>deg. of pos. camber built in to make up for<br>the neg. camber you create when lowering<br>the car. The part# from GM are (#14094449<br>left and #14094450 right). They also claim<br>they get rid of bump steer. Thought i would<br>post this in case anyone was interested.
Z||000009||ccmarsig||03-09-99||12:40 PM||||How much problems do ya'll have with the lowered vehicles?  From the factory, mine is pretty low, though I don't know if it was intended to be this way as it was one of the prototypes for the 87 year (which just means that my mechanic has learned to visually inspect parts before ordering as I've got a few nonstandard parts.<p>Right now, I keep knocking off my front skirt because everything is too low.  I think I damaged it again driving in the snow today.  Am I the only one who does this?  or should I have a winter car as well.  I want the thing lowered, but I'm afraid I'm going to cause more damage than its worth.  I know the skirt acts as an airdamn forcing air into the radiator but if I removed this, is there any other (visually asthetic) way to route air back up here...hell if there was a way just to get a more forgiving aftermarket replacement, I might do this as well.<p>clifyt (the other cliff)
Z||000010||cmore||03-11-99||07:55 PM||||So you think you Fiero is low Cliff? :)<p>I know exactly how you feel with feeling every bump in the road but I'll put up with it because it handles so well. I've had my 2m4 up to 125mph and you said it right it's so smooth you could drive with one finger on the wheel. I lowered my 2 1/2" last year and I measured the hight of the bottom of the air damn to the ground and it came out to be 1 7/8" off the ground. You have to be super careful pulling into anywhere and can become a pain at times. <p>If you want to see pictures just hit my page at:<p>http://frontpage.erie.net/cmore/
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||03-12-99||12:40 AM||||Wow! That <i>is</i> low! I've never actually measured how low mine is, but I'm pretty sure it's higher than yours!<p>The weather finally cleared today, so I'll make some pictures of my Fiero and post them here.
Z||000012||raceknb||03-18-99||07:52 PM||||The stock fiero for the most part has 5" of ground clearance from the factory.  This is low compared to standard cars, but by no means a *ground kisser*  2 1/2" is a bit low for my taste.  I wnet with the 1 1/2" Koni package from THE FIERO STORE complete with ft and rr sway bars.  I have a full poly kit in the frt and rr and am currently testing 225/50zr16 ft and 245/45zr16 rr and have no trouble with clearance.<br>If you change the gactory computer chip with the ADS model, the top speed limiter will go away, and you can experiance the thrill of speed in excess of 130mph(V6 model)  I have been clocked in mine at 142mph and that was at 4400rpm in 5th.  Dont know how much more I can squeeze. These cars do have a bit of hi speed unstability in stock form, but anything over 1" will improve you handeling DRASTICALLY.
